# Target C-II — `t<=1.844` forces a complete `0.003D x 0.35D` center/leaf train rectangle

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only phase-resolved structural strengthening, corrected to the audited dual-tree topology  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** smallest-leaf O--O pair-weight theorem; `>0.73D` weighted center-fan theorem; audited dual-tree train-grid extraction  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current frontier already places a macroscopic center fan on one smallest-leaf
outer-to-outer train.  The same quantitative input forces a **complete
linear-by-linear crossing rectangle** after one dual-tree backbone extraction.

An earlier draft of this note incorrectly said that an arbitrary pairwise noncrossing
family of leaf crosscuts has a globally linear complement.  The repository's audited
phase-grid correction explicitly rejects that statement: the complement dual is a
tree, not necessarily a path.  The proof below uses only the audited tree statement.

Let `Q_V` be the retained weighted O--O family in the smallest leaf phase of the
dominant exact `pi/3` orbit.  Write `w_tau in (0,1]` for the retained pair weight of
`tau`, and let `d_tau D` be the number of center-phase complete trains crossing it.
The parent theorems give, uniformly for every survivor with `t<=1.844`,

\[
P_V:={1\over D}\sum_{\tau\in Q_V}w_\tau>0.0848-o(1),
\tag{R.1}
\]

\[
{\sum_\tau w_\tau d_\tau D\over P_VD}
>0.73D-o(D),
\tag{R.2}
\]

and the center phase has size

\[
C D<1.063D+o(D).
\tag{R.3}
\]

This note proves that there are subfamilies `L_*` of smallest-leaf O--O trains and
`C_*` of center-phase complete trains such that

\[
\boxed{|L_*|>0.003D-o(D),}
\tag{R.4}
\]

\[
\boxed{|C_*|>0.35D-o(D),}
\tag{R.5}
\]

and **every train in `L_*` crosses every train in `C_*`**.

Thus the live `1.84355...<=t<=1.844` band contains an actual complete topological
rectangle with both shores linear in `D`.  This supplies the large center-by-O--O
rectangle explicitly requested by the preceding smallest-leaf frontier note.

---

## 1. More than `0.0275D` weighted leaf cuts have center degree above `0.57D`

Call a retained leaf train **good** when

\[
d_\tau>0.57.
\]

Let

\[
G_D={1\over D}\sum_{\tau\ {m good}}w_\tau.
\]

Every leaf train is crossed by at most all center trains, so by (R.3)

\[
d_\tau<1.063+o(1).
\]

If `G_D` were small, the weighted average in (R.2) would satisfy

\[
0.73P_V
<0.57(P_V-G_D)+1.063G_D+o(1).
\]

Therefore

\[
G_D>
{(0.73-0.57)P_V\over1.063-0.57}-o(1).
\]

Using (R.1),

\[
G_D>
{0.16(0.0848)\over0.493}-o(1)
={1696\over61625}-o(1)
=0.0275212981\ldots-o(1).
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{G_D>0.0275-o(1).}
\tag{R.6}
\]

Because every retained pair weight satisfies `w_tau<=1`, the number `m_D` of distinct
good leaf trains obeys

\[
\boxed{m_D>0.0275D-o(D).}
\tag{R.7}
\]

Every good leaf train is outer-to-outer, and the good family is pairwise noncrossing
because it lies in one exact leaf phase.

---

## 2. Correct topology: the good-leaf complement dual is a tree

The audited phase-grid correction proves the following safe fact.  For any finite
family of pairwise noncrossing proper crosscuts in a disk, the complement region/arc
dual is a tree.  Apply it to the `m=m_D` good leaf O--O trains and call the resulting
tree `T_L`.  Its `m` edges are the good leaf tracks.

Now take any center complete train `gamma`.  As `gamma` runs through complement
regions, crossing a good leaf train traverses the corresponding edge of `T_L`.
Two train tracks cross at most once, so no dual edge is traversed twice.  An
edge-simple walk in a tree is a simple path.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
N(\gamma):=\{\lambda\in Q_V^{good}:\gamma\pitchfork\lambda\}
\text{ is an edge set of one simple path in }T_L.
}
\tag{R.8}

This remains valid even when `gamma` has a bounded-face terminal: its first and last
regions need not be boundary regions.  Properness is required only for the leaf O--O
crosscuts that define the dual tree.

Double counting good-leaf/center crossings gives

\[
I:=\sum_\gamma |N(\gamma)|
=\sum_{\lambda\ good}\deg(\lambda)
>0.57mD-o(D^2).
\tag{R.9}
\]

There are at most

\[
N_C<1.063D+o(D)
\tag{R.10}
\]

center trains.

---

## 3. One center train creates a long dual-tree backbone

Average (R.9) over the center trains.  Some center train `gamma_0` has crossing path
`P_0=N(gamma_0)` of length

\[
|P_0|
>{0.57mD\over1.063D}-o(D)
>{0.57(0.0275)\over1.063}D-o(D).
\]

The exact coefficient is

\[
{627\over42520}=0.01474600188\ldots .
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{|P_0|>0.014746D-o(D).}
\tag{R.11}

Order the leaf edges of this simple path as

\[
\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_s.
\]

For any other center train `gamma`, both `N(gamma)` and `P_0` are simple paths in the
same tree.  The intersection of two tree paths is connected.  Consequently

\[
N(\gamma)\cap P_0
\]

is a consecutive interval in the ordered backbone `lambda_1,...,lambda_s`.

This is exactly the interval structure needed below, and it is now obtained from the
audited dual-tree theorem rather than from the false global-stack shortcut.

---

## 4. Consecutive-block averaging on the backbone

Every backbone leaf edge is good, so each `lambda_i` is crossed by more than
`0.57D-o(D)` center trains.  Therefore the total center/backbone incidence is

\[
I_0>0.57sD-o(D^2).
\tag{R.12}

Fix an integer block length `r`.  If a center train meets `ell_gamma` consecutive
backbone edges, it contains exactly

\[
\max\{\ell_\gamma-r+1,0\}
\]

consecutive `r`-edge backbone blocks.  Since

\[
\max\{\ell-r+1,0\}\ge \ell-(r-1),
\]

summing over all center trains gives

\[
\sum_B \#\{\gamma:B\subset N(\gamma)\cap P_0\}
\ge I_0-(r-1)N_C.
\tag{R.13}

Choose

\[
r=\lfloor0.003D\rfloor.
\]

By (R.11), `r<s` for all sufficiently large `D`.  There are `s-r+1<=s` such
blocks, so some block `B_*` has a common center neighborhood of size at least

\[
{I_0-(r-1)N_C\over s}
>
\left[
0.57-
{0.003(1.063)\over627/42520}
\right]D-o(D).
\]

The exact rational coefficient is

\[
{1848281\over5225000}
=0.3537379904\ldots .
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{|N(B_*)|>0.35D-o(D).}
\tag{R.14}

Take

\[
L_*=B_*,
\qquad
C_*=N(B_*).
\]

Every center train in `C_*` crosses every leaf train in `L_*`, proving
(R.4)--(R.5).

---

## 5. Exact arithmetic certificate

Run

`python3 research/verify_target_c_small_leaf_complete_rectangle_t1844.py`.

It uses only `Fraction` arithmetic and verifies

```text
weighted good-cut density  > 1696/61625 = 0.02752129817...
safe good-cut density      = 11/400     = 0.0275
safe backbone density      = 627/42520  = 0.01474600188...
block leaf density          = 3/1000     = 0.003
common center density       > 1848281/5225000 = 0.3537379904...
safe common center density = 7/20       = 0.35
```

The exact margins include

\[
{1696\over61625}-{11\over400}
={21\over986000}>0,
\]

and

\[
{1848281\over5225000}-{7\over20}
={19531\over5225000}>0.
\]

No floating-point sign is load-bearing.

---

## 6. Interaction with Section 157

Every member of `L_*` is outer-to-outer.  Section 157 therefore gives, for each one,
actual diameter edges crossing its cut with both transverse signs.  The cuts now lie
on one audited dual-tree backbone path, and the same `>0.35D-o(D)` center trains cross
**all** of them.

Consequently the live object is

```text
>0.003D ordered smallest-leaf O--O cuts on one dual-tree backbone
x
>0.35D common exact-center crossing trains
+
two-sided actual-diameter KKT bracing on every leaf cut.
```

For two leaf cuts in this backbone block, subtracting their Section-157 shore-resultant
identities cancels every diameter edge whose endpoints lie on the same side of both
cuts.  The difference is supported on diameter edges interacting with the dual-tree
strip between the cuts.  The complete center rectangle supplies a common ordered
coordinate system across all these strips.

The next source-level target is to bound how many consecutive backbone strips one
straight diameter edge can support with the required transverse sign pattern.  Such a
reuse bound would convert repeated Section-157 bracing into a lower bound on distinct
diameter edges.

---

## 7. Scope and non-implications

- The original global-linear-stack argument is explicitly withdrawn; only the audited
  dual-tree/path-intersection theorem is used.
- The theorem uses the already-proved `t<=1.844` fan and pair-weight inputs; it does not
  improve the low-`t` scalar floor by itself.
- Pair weights are used only to extract many distinct good leaf trains.  The final
  rectangle is unweighted.
- Center trains need not be outer-to-outer for their crossing sets to be paths in the
  leaf complement dual.
- No Target-B trimmed-grid global-minimizer theorem is imported: arbitrary third-phase
  and external-train cancellation remains uncontrolled at this stage.
- No claim is made that the Section-157 diameter bracing edges are distinct across leaf
  cuts.

**Disposition:** corrected proof-only complete-rectangle extraction; C-II and Level I remain open.
